<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:system="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime">

    <!--
        THE LOOK OF THE COMPONENT CATALOGUE, and it has a file of its own for a reason that is
        measured rather than stylistic.

        Values.xaml stands at 376 lines against a markup ceiling of 376, and so does Colours.xaml,
        and so does MainWindow.xaml - measured 2026-09-10 by tools/margin.ps1, which is the tool
        that first said THREE files are on that ceiling rather than one. Backlog 173 is the open
        decision about it. So a screen that needs four numbers of its own cannot put them where
        every other number lives, and the honest answer is a file rather than a raw value in a
        view, which rule 1 of the GUI rules forbids outright.

        EVERY KEY HERE IS A COMPONENT THE CATALOGUE ITSELF WILL SHOW, which is not a curiosity: a
        catalogue that could not draw its own furniture would be a catalogue with an exception in
        it, and an exception is where drift starts.

        WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: colour. The sheet is drawn out of SurfacePanel,
        SurfaceRowLine and SurfacePanelEdge, which the window already declares in Surfaces.xaml.
        A developer screen with a palette of its own would be a second palette to keep in step.
    -->

    <!--
        The width of the column that holds a component's key. Wide enough for the longest one
        this product has - OverviewQualificationNumberText, 31 characters - because a key cut in
        half is a key nobody can search for.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthCatalogueName">260</system:Double>

    <!--
        THE STATE COLUMNS ARE FIXED RATHER THAN SHARED OUT OF WHAT IS LEFT, and the first version
        was not. With star columns a sample sat two hundred pixels away from its own name, because
        a left aligned control in a wide column is a control with a wide margin - and the eye has
        to travel that gap on every one of fifty five rows.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthCatalogueState">260</system:Double>

    <!--
        WIDE ENOUGH TO WRAP, NARROW ENOUGH TO STAY A COLUMN. The extremes cell holds a line long
        enough to run past any column in the product, so this width is what decides where it wraps.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthCatalogueExtreme">420</system:Double>

    <!--
        A CEILING ON A ROW, and it is the difference between a sheet and a scroll. The first
        version had none: OverviewNumberText is drawn at display size, so its long sample wrapped
        to four lines of forty point text and made one row three hundred pixels tall. A sample
        that overflows this is CLIPPED rather than allowed to push, which is also the honest
        answer - a component given more text than it has room for is clipped in the product too.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightCatalogueSample">96</system:Double>

    <!--
        THE TALLER CEILING, for a sample that does not fit under the one above - since 2026-09-16,
        when the plan sheet turned out to be drawn as two faint edges: its own margin pushed every
        pixel of its content past 96 and the clip took the rest. A sample whose measured height is
        over the row ceiling is given this one instead, and the sheet's model decides which, so a
        component that grows moves itself to the taller row without an edit here. Views in their
        four states (docs/PROJEKT-KATALOG-20260916.md, section 3.3) are always this tall.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightCatalogueTall">320</system:Double>

    <!--
        HOW WIDE A VIEW IS DRAWN ON THE SHEET. A view fills its window in the product, and on a
        sheet measured to content it would ask for the whole screen - so a view sample is given the
        width the window itself is allowed to shrink to, which is the narrowest the product ever
        draws it.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="WidthCatalogueView">640</system:Double>

    <!--
        The height the catalogue window opens at. Its WIDTH is not a number here at all: the window
        sizes itself to the columns above (SizeToContent), which is what keeps the last column from
        being cut by the window's edge - the fault the sheet shipped with from 2026-09-10 to
        2026-09-16, when the sum of the columns had grown past a width written into the window.
    -->
    <system:Double x:Key="HeightCatalogueWindow">900</system:Double>

    <!-- Room around a sample so that two of them side by side are not read as one control. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadCatalogueSample">10,8,10,8</Thickness>

    <!-- The gap between one group of components and the next. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginCatalogueGroup">0,0,0,28</Thickness>

    <!--
        A row on the sheet: a line under it and room to breathe. The line is SurfaceRowLine, the
        same brush the list draws between entries, so the sheet and the product agree about what
        a separator looks like.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CatalogueRow" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceRowLine}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderFooterRule}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The box one sample sits in. Left aligned rather than stretched, because a stretched button
        is a button of a size the product never draws, and the point of this screen is to show
        what ships.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CatalogueSampleBox" TargetType="ContentControl">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />

        <!--
            TOP RATHER THAN CENTRE. On a row whose extremes cell wraps to three lines, a centred
            sample floats away from the name that labels it, and the two stop reading as one row.
        -->
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
        <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="{StaticResource HeightCatalogueSample}" />
        <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />

        <!--
            FOCUSABLE IS FALSE ON THE BOX AND NOT ON WHAT IS INSIDE IT. The samples are real
            controls and a person walking this screen with Tab is exactly how the focus state
            gets looked at - trap docs/10 15 and 16 both live in that walk. What must not take a
            stop is the wrapper, which would put an invisible stop in front of every sample.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False" />

        <!--
            OUT OF THE WAY WHEN THERE IS NOTHING IN IT, and this trigger spent one build in the
            wrong place. It sat in a ContentControl.Resources block inside the cell template,
            where an implicit style reaches the CHILDREN of a control and never the control
            itself - so it could not fire, and nothing said so, because a ContentControl holding
            nothing draws nothing either way. A dead trigger that looks alive is worse than no
            trigger: the next person reads it as the mechanism.
        -->
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Element}" Value="{x:Null}">
                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </DataTrigger>

            <!--
                THE TALLER CEILING FOR A SAMPLE THAT MEASURED PAST THE ROW ONE - decided by the
                sheet's model, which measured it, rather than by a list of tall components here.
            -->
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Tall}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="{StaticResource HeightCatalogueTall}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The card a group of components sits on. The same panel surface the plan uses, so the
        sheet is drawn out of the product rather than beside it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CatalogueGroupCard" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerTile}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PaddingPanel}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginCatalogueGroup}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE CELL OF THE SHEET: a sample, or the reason there is none, never both and never
        neither. The two triggers are the whole mechanism and they hang off the empty string
        rather than off null, which is why Catalogue.Sample carries an empty Instead where it has
        an element - the same shape EmptyState.xaml uses two files away.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueCell">
        <Grid>
            <ContentControl Content="{Binding Element}" Style="{StaticResource CatalogueSampleBox}" />

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Instead}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Instead}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE COMPONENT, ACROSS ITS ARRANGEMENTS. The name column is shared across every group with
        SharedSizeGroup, so the sheet reads as one table rather than as a stack of little ones -
        the same mechanism DetailsView.xaml uses for its label column.

        FIVE STATE COLUMNS AND NOT SEVEN. There is no "focused" column and no "hover" column,
        and leaving them out is the deliberate half of this design - the owner's decision of
        2026-09-16, the one exception to GUI rule 4: nothing here can give a control the keyboard
        or put the pointer over it, and a column built by forcing a style's trigger setters would
        show what OUR style says and not what the library's template does underneath, which is a
        picture that can differ from the real hover. Both are looked at by pressing Tab and moving
        the pointer on this screen, which is also what makes the ring in Chips.xaml and
        Controls.xaml visible at all. The fifth column, "chosen", arrived 2026-09-16.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueEntry">
        <Border Style="{StaticResource CatalogueRow}">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueName" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueNormal" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueDisabled" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueWrong" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueChosen" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueName}">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Key}" Style="{StaticResource FixedText}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Target}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="1" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                                  Content="{Binding Normal}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="2" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                                  Content="{Binding Disabled}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
                <!--
                    THE WRONG STATE, A FOURTH COLUMN SINCE 2026-09-15, when the box of seconds
                    became the first component that can be wrong. Built by the sheet's model out of
                    the style's own trigger on Validation.HasError, so a component that learns to be
                    wrong appears here without an edit to this file.
                -->
                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="3" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                                  Content="{Binding Wrong}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
                <!--
                    THE CHOSEN STATE, A FIFTH COLUMN SINCE 2026-09-16. Built by the sheet's model out
                    of the triggers a style or its template carries on IsChecked and IsSelected, so a
                    component that learns to be chosen appears here without an edit to this file.
                -->
                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="4" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                                  Content="{Binding Chosen}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="5" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueExtreme}"
                                  HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                  Content="{Binding Extreme}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        THE FOUR COLUMN NAMES, ONCE PER GROUP RATHER THAN ONCE PER SHEET. A heading at the very
        top scrolls away after the first file, and then every column below it is unlabelled - which
        on a sheet whose whole point is comparing arrangements is the one thing that must not
        happen.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueHeadings">
        <Grid Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueName" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueNormal" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueDisabled" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueWrong" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="CatalogueChosen" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueName}"
                       Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeadingText}" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                       Text="{Binding Normally}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                       Text="{Binding Off}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                       Text="{Binding WhenWrong}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueState}"
                       Text="{Binding WhenChosen}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />

            <TextBlock Grid.Column="5" Text="{Binding TooMuch}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE STATE OF A VIEW, WITH ITS NAME ABOVE IT - the unit of the stacked layout. A label and
        a cell, and the cell is the same CatalogueCell every other sample sits in.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueLabelled">
        <StackPanel>
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Heading}"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource PadCatalogueSample}" />
            <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding Sample}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueCell}" />
        </StackPanel>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE VIEW, ITS STATES ONE UNDER THE NEXT - since 2026-09-16, when the views joined the
        sheet. A view is as wide as the narrowest window the product draws it in, and five of
        those abreast would be wider than any screen this project has measured on, so the states
        of a view are stacked and each carries its own heading. The states come from the model as
        a LIST (Entry.Stacked), and the list is drawn by one template rather than five copies of a
        block - GUI rule 8, which counts three copies as one too many.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueViewEntry">
        <Border Style="{StaticResource CatalogueRow}">
            <StackPanel>
                <StackPanel Width="{StaticResource WidthCatalogueName}" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Key}" Style="{StaticResource FixedText}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Target}" Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Stacked}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueLabelled}"
                              Focusable="False" />
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE THEME FILE, AND EVERY COMPONENT IT DECLARES - or the views, stacked. Which of the two
        row templates a group is drawn with is the group's own word (IsStacked), read here by a
        trigger, and a stacked group hides the column headings its rows carry themselves.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="CatalogueGroup">
        <Border Style="{StaticResource CatalogueGroupCard}">
            <StackPanel>
                <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding}" ContentTemplate="{StaticResource CatalogueHeadings}">
                    <ContentPresenter.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="ContentPresenter">
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsStacked}" Value="True">
                                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </ContentPresenter.Style>
                </ContentPresenter>

                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Entries}" Focusable="False">
                    <ItemsControl.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="ItemsControl">
                            <Setter Property="ItemTemplate" Value="{StaticResource CatalogueEntry}" />
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsStacked}" Value="True">
                                    <Setter Property="ItemTemplate" Value="{StaticResource CatalogueViewEntry}" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </ItemsControl.Style>
                </ItemsControl>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

</ResourceDictionary>
